You can parse the abstract syntax tree (AST) of the transformation function to extract column names before execution. This avoids manual registration and complex proxy objects.

The following function inspects the source code of a transformation, finds `df["col"]` and `df.col` patterns, and classifies them as reads or writes based on context (e.g., assignment targets are writes).

```python
import ast

def track_io(func):
    tree = ast.parse(inspect.getsource(func))
    reads, writes = set(), set()

    for node in ast.walk(tree):
        if isinstance(node, ast.Subscript):
            if isinstance(node.value, ast.Name) and node.value.id == 'df':
                col = ast.literal_eval(node.slice)
                if isinstance(node.ctx, ast.Load):
                    reads.add(col)
                elif isinstance(node.ctx, ast.Store):
                    writes.add(col)
    return reads, writes

def T1(df):
    df.loc[(df["col1"] > 4) & (df["col3"] == 2), "col2"] = 3

print(track_io(T1))  # Output: ({'col1', 'col3'}, {'col2'})
```

This approach resolves variables statically. Note that it requires the column names to be string literals in the code.
